From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 14:32:31 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 060/342] net notifications: stay loaded, fixing a deadlock at
 boot

The notifications module unloaded with the network stack and kept itself
loaded while it had listeners, by getting a reference to itself when the
first was added (from within its generic syscall). That deadlocked: the
stack's last user (net_server closing its last socket) put the stack
while holding the module lock, the stack put us, and our uninit waited in
unregister_generic_syscall() for a thread in our syscall (launch_daemon
starting to watch the network) that was adding the first listener and
waited for the module lock in get_module(). Seen once in about 30 boots
of an ODROID-C2: a hung boot, no network, no desktop.

The module is now B_KEEP_LOADED: its syscall is never unregistered, and
adding a listener no longer takes the module lock. That also settles the
race the uninit's TODO described. Listeners now survive the stack being
unloaded and loaded again.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../network/notifications/notifications.cpp   | 41 +++++++------------
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 27 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/add-ons/kernel/network/notifications/notifications.cpp b/src/add-ons/kernel/network/notifications/notifications.cpp
index cdb7609d55..664ad2956e 100644
--- a/src/add-ons/kernel/network/notifications/notifications.cpp
+++ b/src/add-ons/kernel/network/notifications/notifications.cpp
@@ -3,7 +3,18 @@
  * Distributed under the terms of the MIT License.
  */
 
-/*! Provides the networking stack notification service. */
+/*! Provides the networking stack notification service.
+
+	The module stays loaded once it was loaded (B_KEEP_LOADED). It used to
+	unload with the stack, and to keep itself loaded while it had listeners
+	by getting a reference to itself when the first one was added. That
+	deadlocked: the stack's last user put it, holding the module lock, and
+	the stack put us; our uninit then waited in unregister_generic_syscall()
+	for a thread inside our syscall that was adding the first listener and
+	waited for the module lock in get_module() (seen at boot, between
+	net_server closing its last socket and launch_daemon starting to watch
+	the network).
+*/
 
 #include <net_notifications.h>
 
@@ -29,8 +40,6 @@ public:
 
 protected:
 	virtual	void				LastReferenceReleased();
-	virtual	void				FirstAdded();
-	virtual	void				LastRemoved();
 };
 
 
@@ -72,24 +81,6 @@ NetNotificationService::LastReferenceReleased()
 }
 
 
-void
-NetNotificationService::FirstAdded()
-{
-	// The reference counting doesn't work for us, as we'll have to
-	// ensure our module stays loaded.
-	module_info* dummy;
-	get_module(NET_NOTIFICATIONS_MODULE_NAME, &dummy);
-}
-
-
-void
-NetNotificationService::LastRemoved()
-{
-	// Give up the reference _AddListener()
-	put_module(NET_NOTIFICATIONS_MODULE_NAME);
-}
-
-
 //	#pragma mark - User generic syscall
 
 
@@ -144,14 +135,10 @@ notifications_std_ops(int32 op, ...)
 			return B_OK;
 		}
 		case B_MODULE_UNINIT:
+			// not called: the module is kept loaded
 			TRACE("uninit\n");
 
 			unregister_generic_syscall(NET_NOTIFICATIONS_SYSCALLS, 1);
-
-			// TODO: due to the way the locking in the notification
-			// manager works, there's a potential race condition here
-			// where someone attempts to add a listener right as
-			// we're uninitializing. Needs to be looked at/resolved.
 			sNotificationService.Unregister();
 
 			// we need to release the reference that was acquired
@@ -169,7 +156,7 @@ notifications_std_ops(int32 op, ...)
 net_notifications_module_info sNotificationsModule = {
 	{
 		NET_NOTIFICATIONS_MODULE_NAME,
-		0,
+		B_KEEP_LOADED,
 		notifications_std_ops
 	},
 
